.TH BOTFERENCE 1 "July 2026" "botference" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
botference \- a multi-LLM planning council for the terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B botference
[\fIoptions\fR] [\fBinit\fR|\fBplan\fR|\fBresearch-plan\fR|\fBarchive\fR|\fBbuild\fR] [\fIiterations\fR]
.br
.B botference plan
[\fB\-\-web\fR|\fB\-\-share\fR [\fB\-\-no-auth\fR]] [\fB\-\-port\fR \fIN\fR]
.br
.B botference review
[\fIdir\fR] [\fB\-\-share\fR [\fB\-\-service\fR]] [\fB\-\-hosted\fR] [\fB\-\-port\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-no-agents\fR] [\fB\-\-upgrade\fR]
.br
.B botference discuss
[\fB\-\-port\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-service\fR] [\fB\-\-no-agents\fR] [\fB\-\-hosted\fR] [\fB\-\-share\fR [\fB\-\-service\fR]] [\fB\-\-here\fR]
.br
.B botference discuss
\fB\-\-install-autostart\fR | \fB\-\-uninstall-autostart\fR
.br
.B botference discuss
\fB\-\-install-tunnel\fR | \fB\-\-uninstall-tunnel\fR
.br
.B botference service
\fBstart\fR \fIname\fR \fB\-\-\fR \fIcommand\fR… | \fBstop\fR \fIname\fR|\fB\-\-all\fR | \fBlist\fR | \fBlogs\fR \fIname\fR [\fB\-n\fR \fIN\fR]
.br
.B botference see
\fIurl\fR|\fB:\fR\fIport\fR|\fIservice-name\fR [\fIlabel\fR] [\fB\-\-viewport\fR \fIWxH\fR]… [\fB\-\-basic-auth\fR \fIU:P\fR] [\fB\-\-out\fR \fIdir\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B botference
puts you, Claude Code, and Codex in one terminal room. The bots debate
each other in free-form exchanges \- handing the floor back and forth on a
budget \- while you steer, until the council converges on an
implementation plan written by an agreed lead.
.PP
Planning sessions are persistent: chats are saved, resumable, and can be
filed under projects. Pre-existing native Claude Code chats can be adopted
into the council with
.BR /adopt .
.SH MODES
.TP
.B init
Bootstrap a project-local state directory.
.TP
.B plan
Free-form planning council: you + Claude + Codex in one room; the bots can
hand each other the floor (budgeted) until the conversation returns to you.
With \fB\-\-web\fR the council serves as a chat web app in the browser
instead of the Ink TUI (sidebar where every project expands to its recent
chats and tapping any chat opens it \- no "make active project" step \-
streaming transcript, slash commands with autocomplete, choice/permission
cards);
\fB\-\-share\fR additionally puts it behind an in-page password gate
(COUNCIL_PASSWORD, generated when unset) and a cloudflared tunnel, and
prints one shareable https URL plus the password \- open it on a phone.
\fB\-\-share \-\-no-auth\fR skips the password gate entirely: no password
means anyone with the URL can drive your agents, which can read and write
files on this machine; URLs leak (link previews, chat logs) \- prefer the
default password. \fB\-\-port\fR \fIN\fR picks the web port (default
4187). The web server runs one bridge session per open chat, so browser
tabs on different chats are concurrent sessions (COUNCIL_MAX_CHATS caps
the pool, default 4); do not open the same session in the TUI and the
browser at once.
.TP
.B research-plan
Structured planning with prompts/plan.md.
.TP
.B archive
Archive the current thread and restore blank templates.
.TP
.B build
Build loop (default), optionally capped at \fIiterations\fR.
.TP
.B review \fR[\fIdir\fR]
Set up and serve the document-review interface (Google-Docs-style
comments on rendered LaTeX/Markdown, with the bots replying in threads)
in a document repo; \fIdir\fR defaults to the current directory. The
first run copies the engine into \fIdir\fR/review/, auto-detects the
document configuration into review/review.config.json (echoed for
eyeballing), adds the gitignore block, and builds the site; every run
serves it until Ctrl-C. Requires \fBpandoc\fR. The agent bridge turns on
automatically when the machine can run it \- python3 plus at least one
agent CLI (claude or codex) on PATH \- and the launcher prints which
agents it detected; without them the site serves read-and-comment only.
Flags: \fB\-\-port\fR \fIN\fR serves on port \fIN\fR;
\fB\-\-no-agents\fR forces the bridge off and \fB\-\-agents\fR forces it
on (erroring if impossible); \fB\-\-share\fR serves hosted mode behind a
cloudflared tunnel, respecting REVIEW_PASSWORD or generating a
password, and prints one shareable URL (see NAMED TUNNELS below for a
stable URL across sessions);
\fB\-\-hosted\fR enables shared-URL mode without the tunnel
(REVIEW_PASSWORD, entered on an in-page gate that asks for a name and
the password together \(em the name only decides which state/users
file that guest writes; tools may send Basic auth with any username
instead); \fB\-\-upgrade\fR refreshes
engine files
without touching review.config.json, state/, suggestions.json, or site/;
\fB\-\-setup\fR scaffolds and builds, then exits without serving (what
the review hub runs before it can publish a project that was never set
up). \fB\-\-hosted \-\-service\fR runs just the hosted server (no
tunnel) under the managed service lifecycle, named after the directory
unless \fB\-\-service-name\fR \fINAME\fR says otherwise; its ledger
belongs to the paper's own directory, so stop it from there.
.TP
.B discuss
Serve \fBDiscuss\fR, the companion server for the browser extension.
\fBplugin\fR is an accepted alias and remains one: the product was
renamed, but the command, the \fI.botference/plugin\fR data directories
and the \fBcom.botference.plugin-*\fR LaunchAgent labels were not, so
nothing anyone typed or stored has to change.
(load \fIfrontends/plugin/extension\fR once via the browser's
"Load unpacked"): highlight text on any static article page, leave
comments, and \- when a comment or reply mentions \fB@claude\fR,
\fB@codex\fR, or \fB@all\fR \- the bots answer inline in the same
thread, streamed into the extension's right-side drawer (Comments and
Page chat tabs). Every page exports to one Obsidian note; bot
conversations persist as council chats under the "Plugin pages"
project, titled by the article's headline. Run it from your main
workspace so those chats land in that workspace's council. Agents
auto-detect exactly as in \fBreview\fR. Flags: \fB\-\-port\fR \fIN\fR
(default 4189, which the extension expects); \fB\-\-no-agents\fR /
\fB\-\-agents\fR force the bridge off/on; \fB\-\-service\fR runs the
companion detached under the managed service lifecycle as
\fBplugin-web\fR (stop with \fBbotference service stop plugin-web\fR).
Config (Obsidian vault path, export folder) in
\fI.botference/plugin/config.json\fR. The workspace is sticky: after the
first run, \fBbotference discuss\fR from any directory reuses the
recorded workspace (\fB\-\-here\fR forces the current directory).
\fB\-\-hosted\fR (requires PLUGIN_PASSWORD) adds a password gate with
per-guest handles so collaborators see and comment on the same threads;
guest @-mentions need a grant in
\fI.botference/plugin/grants.json\fR (re-read live; daily caps).
Extension-less guests get a server-rendered reading room at
\fI/pages\fR, and any page whose article has been captured also has an
ARTICLE VIEW at \fI/a/<pageKey>\fR: the prose itself with the
highlights painted in place, tap one for its thread, select text to
start a new one, ask the bots, export. The extension sends that
snapshot when a page is first annotated and again whenever the prose
changes; the companion sanitizes it against an allowlist (no scripts,
iframes, event handlers or non-http links survive) and serves it under
a strict Content-Security-Policy. Highlights made from a phone are
ordinary threads \- the article view runs the extension's own
\fIanchor.js\fR \- so they appear in the page itself on the Mac. A page
annotated before snapshots existed says so and offers its comments
instead.
.IP
Owner identity is shared with the review documents rather than
duplicated: a browser already approved as an owner DEVICE for the
review hub is the owner here too, with nothing typed (the hub scopes
its \fBhub_device\fR cookie to the parent domain and the companion
verifies it with the hub's own secret), and the owner PASSWORD is
resolved exactly as \fBhub.mjs\fR resolves it \-
\fBPLUGIN_OWNER_PASSWORD\fR, then \fBREVIEW_HUB_PASSWORD\fR, then
\fI.owner\fR in \fI~/.botference/review-paper-secrets.json\fR
(generated and persisted there on first use), which is the same value
the hub hands every paper as \fBREVIEW_OWNER_PASSWORD\fR. An owner
authenticated either way has FULL owner rights remotely. Sessions are
signed cookies carrying the role AND the name, last 30 days and renew
themselves in use; \fI/signout\fR ends one.
\fB\-\-share\fR is hosted mode plus a cloudflared quick
tunnel (generates PLUGIN_PASSWORD when unset, prints one shareable URL);
with \fB\-\-service\fR the share runs detached as \fBplugin-share\fR.
Owner-only regardless of auth: export, page deletion, model/effort/
verbosity, relay, interrupt.
.IP
\fB\-\-install-autostart\fR (macOS) makes the companion permanent
instead of per-session: it writes
\fI~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.botference.plugin-web.plist\fR for the
workspace you run it from and loads it immediately
(\fBlaunchctl bootstrap gui/$UID\fR, falling back to
\fBlaunchctl load -w\fR), so the companion runs at every login and
restarts if it dies. Any \fB\-\-port\fR/\fB\-\-no-agents\fR given
alongside is baked in, together with a PATH that can still find
\fBnode\fR, \fBpython3\fR, and the agent CLIs (launchd agents start
with a minimal PATH). Output is appended to
\fI<workspace>/.botference/logs/plugin-autostart.log\fR; check the job
with \fBlaunchctl list | grep com.botference.plugin-web\fR. A companion
you run by hand still wins: it holds the workspace lock, the launchd
copy exits and is retried about every 10 seconds, so it takes over
within ~10s of your Ctrl\-C. \fB\-\-uninstall-autostart\fR boots the job
out and deletes the plist (a no-op if nothing was installed). Neither
flag may be combined with \fB\-\-service\fR.
.IP
\fB\-\-install-tunnel\fR (macOS, plus a domain whose DNS is on
Cloudflare) replaces \fB\-\-share\fR's disposable
\fItrycloudflare.com\fR URL with one permanent address you can bookmark
on a phone. It creates \- or reuses, so it is safe to re-run \- the
named tunnel \fBbotference-plugin\fR, routes
\fIdiscuss.botference.com\fR to it (and keeps
\fImemorizer.botference.com\fR, where the question vault's quiz is served at
\fI/\fR, and \fIplugin.botference.com\fR, the address from before the rename, routed
and served by the same companion through a second ingress rule, so old
bookmarks and configured extensions still land), writes
\fI~/.cloudflared/botference-plugin.yml\fR (one ingress rule to
\fIhttp://127.0.0.1:4189\fR plus the catch-all cloudflared insists on),
installs a second LaunchAgent \fBcom.botference.plugin-tunnel\fR that
runs the tunnel at every login (unconditional \fBKeepAlive\fR: a tunnel
that exits because the edge hung up must still come back), and rebuilds
the companion's own LaunchAgent with \fB\-\-hosted\fR, preserving the
\fB\-\-port\fR/\fB\-\-no-agents\fR it was installed with. The shared
password is generated once \- four words and a number \- and kept in
\fI~/.botference/plugin-password\fR (mode 0600); it is never written
into a plist, because launchd runs the launcher and \fB\-\-hosted\fR
with no \fBPLUGIN_PASSWORD\fR in the environment reads that file. The
URL and the password are printed at the end. Override the address with
\fBBOTFERENCE_PLUGIN_HOSTNAME\fR, the legacy one with
\fBBOTFERENCE_PLUGIN_LEGACY_HOSTNAME\fR (empty to drop it), and the
tunnel name with \fBBOTFERENCE_PLUGIN_TUNNEL\fR. No inbound port is opened: cloudflared
dials out. This machine stays the unauthenticated owner on
\fI127.0.0.1\fR while everything arriving through the tunnel is a
guest \- told apart by the \fBHost\fR header and by the \fBCF-*\fR /
\fBX-Forwarded-*\fR headers Cloudflare stamps on, since cloudflared's
own hop to the companion is loopback too.
.IP
\fB\-\-uninstall-tunnel\fR boots out and deletes the tunnel LaunchAgent
and puts the companion back to plain localhost mode. The Cloudflare
tunnel and the DNS record are deliberately left in place so
re-installing is one command; the output names the
\fBcloudflared tunnel delete botference-plugin\fR that removes them
from the account as well. Neither tunnel flag may be combined with
\fB\-\-service\fR, \fB\-\-hosted\fR, \fB\-\-share\fR, or the autostart
flags \- it is a one-off install, not a way to start a server.
.TP
.B service
Managed long-lived processes (dev servers, share tunnels) that survive
the shell \- and the whole process group \- that started them: the
sanctioned, auditable way for agents inside botference sessions to
leave something running after their turn ends (anything they background
with a bare \fB&\fR or \fBnohup\fR dies with the turn's process-group
teardown).
.B start
\fIname\fR \fB\-\-\fR \fIcommand\fR… forks \fIcommand\fR into its own
session and process group with stdin from /dev/null and stdout+stderr
appended to \fI.botference/logs/service-<name>.log\fR (rotated to .1
past ~5MB), records {name, pid, pgid, command, started, cwd, log} in
the per-workspace ledger \fI.botference/services.json\fR (atomic
writes), and refuses duplicate running names; stale entries with dead
pids are reaped on every invocation. \fIname\fR is [a-z0-9-], max 32
chars.
.B stop
\fIname\fR (or \fB\-\-all\fR) TERMs the service's process group,
escalates to KILL after 5 seconds, and removes the ledger entry.
.B list
prints every service in every registered ledger (name, pid, uptime,
alive/dead, directory, command, log path) \- a global view that works
from any directory, fed by the self-maintained index
\fI~/.botference/ledgers\fR. \fBstop\fR and \fBlogs\fR remain scoped to
the current directory's ledger; run them from the directory shown in
the DIR column.
.B logs
\fIname\fR [\fB\-n\fR \fIN\fR] tails the service log (default 50
lines).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-p
Non-interactive (pipe) mode.
.TP
.BI \-\-anthropic-model= name
Override the Anthropic model (Claude participant).
.TP
.B \-\-claude
Solo Claude mode (skip Codex).
.TP
.B \-\-claude-interactive
Experimental: mirror a real interactive Claude Code session through tmux;
botference becomes the steering layer over an attachable session.
.TP
.BI \-\-claude-transport= programmatic|tmux
Select the Claude transport for planning mode.
.TP
.B \-\-web
Plan mode only: serve the council chat web app locally instead of the
Ink TUI and print the URL (nothing opens automatically).
.TP
.B \-\-share
Plan mode only: \fB\-\-web\fR plus the password gate and a cloudflared
tunnel; prints "share this: <url>   password: <pw>".
.TP
.B \-\-service
With \fB\-\-share\fR (both \fBplan\fR and \fBreview\fR): run the
whole share (server + tunnel) under the managed service lifecycle
instead of in the foreground \- the launcher waits (bounded) for the
canonical "share this: <url>   password: <pw>" line to appear in the
service log, prints it, and returns control with the share still up.
Service names: \fBcouncil-share\fR (plan) and \fBreview-share\fR
(review); stop with \fBbotference service stop\fR \fIname\fR.
Re-running while the service is up reprints its last share line. This
is the flag agents should use to stand up a share that outlives their
turn. With \fBreview \-\-hosted\fR (no tunnel) it instead runs the one
hosted server as a service \- see \fB\-\-service-name\fR \- which is
how the review hub's on/off toggles publish a paper.
.TP
.B \-\-no-auth
With \fBplan \-\-share\fR only: skip the password gate. Never the
default. Prints a prominent warning at launch and shows a dismissible
warning banner in the served page: no password means anyone with the
URL can drive your agents, which can read and write files on this
machine; URLs leak. Prefer the default password.
.TP
.BI \-\-port " N"
Port for \fBplan \-\-web\fR/\fB\-\-share\fR (default: 4187).
.TP
.BI \-\-project-dir= slug
Project-local state directory slug (default: botference).
.TP
.BI \-\-run-tag= tag
Tag for this run (used in logs).
.TP
.BI \-\-profile= name
Init profile (default: vault-drafter).
.TP
.B \-\-help
Show launcher help and exit.
.SH SEE (AGENT EYES)
.B botference see
renders a page in headless Chrome (the system browser \- no Playwright,
no install) and writes one PNG per viewport, printing each path. Made
for agents: layout and design failures produce no errors or warnings,
so an agent that only reads code and logs will ship a page that "works"
but looks broken \- after any UI change, look at it. The default
viewports are 390x844 (phone) and 1440x900 (desktop); \fB\-\-viewport
WxH\fR (repeatable) replaces them. The target may be a full URL, a bare
\fB:port\fR on localhost, an existing local file (HTML, SVG \- rendered
via file:// with no server), or the name of a running \fBbotference
service\fR \- the listening port is discovered from the live process,
so agents never need to know ports. Output lands in
\fI.botference/shots/\fR unless \fB\-\-out\fR says otherwise.
.PP
Agent sandboxes cannot launch Chrome; the same command hands the
request to the \fBsee-broker\fR automatically \- a ledgered service
(\fBbotference service start see-broker \-\- botference see \-\-serve\fR)
that watches every registered workspace's \fI.botference/see/\fR spool
from outside the sandbox and answers through the filesystem with
identical output. No sandbox is loosened.
.SH IN-SESSION COMMANDS
Inside a planning session, commands start with a slash. Run
.B /help
in the room for the authoritative list. Highlights:
.TP
.B /new /adopt /file /rename /resume /delete
Chat lifecycle: start fresh, continue a native Claude Code chat here,
file under a project, name, switch, or delete chats (picker-driven).
\fB/resume\fR reaches a saved chat in any project, not just the active
one; opening a chat makes that chat's project the active context, and
\fB/file\fR likewise makes the project you filed into the active one.
A chat remembers its own project: filing is an explicit act
(\fB/file\fR, \fB/project assign\fR, \fB/project open\fR, or
\fB/new --project <id>\fR at creation), and saving a chat while a
different project is open never re-files it. \fB/new --inbox\fR starts a
deliberately unfiled chat even from inside a project.
.TP
.B /archive [<id-prefix>|list] , /unarchive [<id-prefix>]
Archive a saved chat instead of deleting it: the session JSON moves from
\fIwork/sessions/\fR to \fIarchive/sessions/\fR (\fBBOTFERENCE_ARCHIVE_DIR\fR),
leaving every listing while staying on disk. /unarchive moves it back and
never overwrites a live chat of the same id. Both open a picker with no
argument; /archive list shows what is already archived.
.TP
.B /project unfile [<session-id-prefix>]
Take a chat out of its project without deleting anything: the transcript,
the title and the file all stay exactly as they were, and only the filing
goes, so the chat lands back in Inbox and \fB/file\fR puts it wherever
you meant. With no argument it acts on the chat you are in (identical to
\fB/project clear\fR); with a session-id prefix it acts on a saved one,
rewriting that chat's \fIproject_id\fR on disk as well as the index.
The web council sidebar offers it as "Remove from project" in a chat
row's actions menu, above Archive and Delete.
.TP
.B /project contents [<project-id>]
What is actually in a project, read-only: its chats (title, date, id) and
a shallow listing of \fIprojects/<id>/\fR with sizes \(em top level plus
one level inside each folder, and a folder it did not walk into is marked
with a trailing ellipsis. Defaults to the open project. The same two
lists appear behind "contents" in the web council sidebar.
.TP
.B /project github [<project-id>] [<repo-name>]
Make the project's folder a git repo and push it to a \fBnew private
GitHub repo\fR, using your own \fBgh\fR login \(em Botference never
handles a token. Confirm-gated: it checks that gh is installed and logged
in, then names the repo it is about to create and asks; an interface that
cannot ask is refused rather than guessed at. The name defaults to a slug
of the project title. A folder that is already a repo is not
re-initialised, and one that already has an \fIorigin\fR is pushed to
rather than given a second repo. The resulting URL is remembered in
\fIprojects/portfolio.json\fR and shown in the panels.
.TP
.B /project archive <id> , /project unarchive <id>
Tuck a project away or bring it back. Only the \fIstatus\fR field in
\fIprojects/portfolio.json\fR changes \(em the folder and its chats are
untouched. Archived projects sort last and collapse into a closed
"Archived" section in the web council sidebar.
.TP
.B /lead /draft /finalize
Planning: set (or let the bots vote on) the plan writer, draft
implementation-plan.md with AI review rounds, and finalize with a
checkpoint.
.TP
.B /relay /compact /goal /permissions /status /auth /model /effort
Session management, including per-participant model and effort switches.
.B /relay @both
(alias @all, /relay-both) resets both agents at once from one shared
handoff \(em authored once by the agent with the most context headroom
\(em with both restarts running in parallel.
.TP
.B /autorelay [on|off]
Auto-relay a model when its context occupancy crosses 50% of its window,
reusing the same handoff machinery as /relay. On by default; the relay is
deferred to a safe boundary (never mid-turn or mid free-form thread) and
lands before that model's next turn. Persists per-user; a matching toggle
appears in the web council agents panel.
.TP
.B /notify [on|off]
Desktop notification (via your terminal) when the bots finish a turn.
Persists per-user.
.TP
.B /agents [on|off]
Grant/revoke the Claude participant's subagent (Task) tool. Off by
default per chat; Claude suggests it and waits for approval.
.TP
.B /allow-host [<domain>]
Grant the bots' sandbox network access to a site (e.g.
.BR example.org ).
Persists per workspace and applies from each bot's next turn \- no
restarts. Without arguments, lists current grants. The bots are
instructed to ask for this instead of working around a blocked fetch.
.TP
.B /watch <url> [question]
Have Gemini watch a public YouTube video and post the report into the
chat, so Claude and Codex \- neither of which can take video \- can read
it on their next turn. With a question, it is answered first. Needs a
Google AI Studio key in
.I ~/.botference/gemini-key
or
.BR GEMINI_API_KEY .
Any YouTube link in a message is watched automatically (up to three per
message, cached for 30 days); set
.B BOTFERENCE_VIDEO_WATCH=off
to turn that off while keeping this command. A bot can ask for a video
itself by ending a reply with a line reading
.BR "watch: <url>" .
.PP
Plain text is auto-routed;
.BR @claude ,
.BR @codex ,
and
.B @all
address participants directly. Typing while Claude is working
.I steers
its current turn (the message is read after its current tool call, as in
native Claude Code); messages during Codex turns queue for the next turn.
Esc interrupts the current turn.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I <project>/botference/
Project-local state: sessions, projects, plans, logs.
.TP
.I ~/.botference/settings.json
Per-user preferences (e.g. notifications).
.TP
.I ~/.botference/gemini-key
Google AI Studio key used to have YouTube videos watched (see
.BR /watch ).
Keep it readable only by you.
.TP
.I <cwd>/.botference/services.json, <cwd>/.botference/logs/service-<name>.log
Managed-service ledger and per-service logs (see
.BR service ).
Per workspace, gitignored by convention; never committed.
.TP
.I <cwd>/.botference/ink-crash.log, <cwd>/.botference/crash-reports/
UI crash evidence (exceptions; fatal V8/OOM reports). Python-side
exceptions land in <work>/sessions/crash.log. Fresh evidence is
surfaced at the next launch.
.SH NAMED TUNNELS
Both
.B plan --share
and
.B review --share
use a cloudflared quick tunnel by default \- a fresh random
trycloudflare.com URL each session. For a stable URL, create a named
tunnel once:
.PP
.nf
  cloudflared tunnel login
  cloudflared tunnel create <your-tunnel-name>
  cloudflared tunnel route dns <your-tunnel-name> council.example.com
  export BOTFERENCE_TUNNEL=<your-tunnel-name>
  export BOTFERENCE_TUNNEL_URL=https://council.example.com
.fi
.PP
With
.B BOTFERENCE_TUNNEL
set, \-\-share runs \fBcloudflared tunnel run\fR against that tunnel
(mapping it to the local port) instead of a quick tunnel;
.B BOTFERENCE_TUNNEL_URL
is printed as the share URL when set.
Live events (streaming replies, project lists) travel over WebSocket,
which traverses tunnels reliably; server-sent events remain as a
fallback for local use.
.SH REVIEW HUB
.B frontends/review/hub.mjs
is one front door for every hosted paper on the machine, behind ONE
named tunnel: a gated portal on the hub hostname, and one transparent
reverse proxy per paper hostname. Config lives in
.I ~/.botference/review-hub.json
.RB ( REVIEW_HUB_CONFIG
overrides it) and is re-read on every request, so edits and toggles
apply without a restart.
.TP
.B Auto-discovery
With a
.B workspace
key set, every directory under \fIworkspace\fR/projects/ is a review
candidate: \fIscaffolded\fR once it has review/review.config.json,
\fInot set up yet\fR otherwise. The owner portal lists all of them
merged with the explicit \fBpapers\fR entries; an explicit entry wins
any slug or directory collision.
.TP
.B On/off toggles
Turning a paper on scaffolds it if needed (\fBreview --setup\fR),
assigns a free port from
.BR portRange ,
runs \fBcloudflared tunnel route dns review <slug>.<domain>\fR
(tunnel name from
.BR REVIEW_HUB_TUNNEL ,
default \fBreview\fR) and starts it with \fBreview --hosted --service
--port\fR \fIN\fR. A failed DNS route is surfaced with the exact
command to run and is never fatal. Turning it off stops that paper's
service by its ledger entry, from the paper's own directory.
.TP
.B Wake-on-request
An owner asking for a paper whose server is down gets a
self-refreshing "starting…" page and the same start path; guests get
the offline "work from the git repo" page.
.TP
.B Owner devices
Localhost is always the owner. A remote browser can request approval:
the hub fires a macOS notification and dialog, and Approve issues a
one-year HMAC-signed cookie scoped to the parent domain, so that
browser is the owner on the paper subdomains too. Pending requests
expire after five minutes.
.B REVIEW_HUB_PASSWORD
remains the password route from any device. Deleting
.I ~/.botference/.review-hub-device-secret
revokes every approved device at once.
.TP
.B Privacy
A newly enabled paper gets a generated guest password and an empty
.B collaborators
list, so only the owner can see or reach it until collaborators are
declared. Secrets live in
.I ~/.botference/review-paper-secrets.json
(mode 0600), never in the config.
.TP
.B Project files
Every discovered project is browsable, owner-only, at
.IR /p/<slug>/files/ ,
served by the hub process itself \- no scaffolding, no paper server
and no DNS record needed, so bot-produced plots and HTML reports are
viewable at the portal with zero setup. Dot-segment path components
(.git, .botference, any dotfile) and traversal are refused, symlinks
are resolved and re-checked, and a project's own HTML is served under
a sandbox CSP with an opaque origin.
